// =============================================================================
//  probe_game.hml — W4-11's acceptance harness.   THE GAME LOOP, HEADLESS.
// =============================================================================
//
//      hemlockc -O1 tools/probe_game.hml -o /tmp/probe_game && /tmp/probe_game
//      hemlock  tools/probe_game.hml                      # interpreted parity
//      nm /tmp/probe_game | grep -c SDL_                  # MUST be 0
//
//  Exits 1 on any failed assertion, and asserts its own assertion COUNT so a
//  section that silently stops running is a failure rather than a green run.
//
//  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WHY THIS PROBE CAN EXIST AT ALL, AND WHY THAT IS THE POINT
//  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  It links `src/game/server.hml` and `src/game/client.hml` and NOT
//  `src/game/main.hml`, and it therefore links no SDL, opens no window and
//  reads no clock. That is only possible because the loop was split the way
//  BUILD_PLAN W4-11 asked for: the shell owns the window and the clock, the
//  server owns the simulation, the client owns input-to-picture, and the two
//  halves talk over a transport. A monolithic `main.hml` with the game logic
//  inlined could not be tested without a display, which means the 60-second
//  sequence would be tested by hand or not at all.
//
//  The frame clock is a NUMBER here — `p_frame_ms` — exactly as
//  `sim_accum_advance` takes it. That is the whole reason the accumulator is
//  clock-free.
// =============================================================================

